  "summary": "A Brennan Center research report explaining how ALPR technology works, surveying Fourth Amendment and state-law jurisprudence (Carpenter, Jones, Glover, McCarthy, Neal v. Fairfax County, Green v. SF), and detailing policy concerns including error rates, data sharing with ICE, disparate impact, and First Amendment chilling. It concludes with recommendations on retention limits, warrants for historical searches, audits, transparency, and hot list verification.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "99.9 percent of the 320 million images Los Angeles stores are for vehicles that were not on a hot list when the image was made",
      "paragraph_idx": 47
    },
    {
      "quote": "An ACLU investigation found that more than 80 local police departments had set up their LEARN settings to share ALPR data with U.S. Immigrations and Customs Enforcement (ICE), even though the practice may violate local privacy laws or sanctuary policies.",
      "paragraph_idx": 55
    },
    {
      "quote": "with enough cameras in enough locations, the historic location data from an ALPR system in Massachusetts would invade a reasonable expectation of privacy and constitute a search for constitutional purposes",
      "paragraph_idx": 40
    },
    {
      "quote": "one randomized control trial in Vallejo, California, found that 37 percent of all ALPR \"hits\" from fixed readers (such as those attached to a street light) and 35 percent from mobile ALPRs were misreads",
      "paragraph_idx": 50
    }
  ],
